Built with pasha CLI — **Hexagonal / DDD** architecture on NestJS.

## Run

```bash
npm install
npm run start:dev
```

Server comes up at `http://localhost:3000/{{moduleName}}`.

## Structure (Hexagonal layers)

```
src/
├── domain/{{moduleName}}/            ← core: no dependency on NestJS or a database
│   ├── entities/{{moduleName}}.entity.ts
│   └── repositories/{{moduleName}}.repository.interface.ts
├── application/{{moduleName}}/       ← use-cases and DTOs
│   ├── use-cases/
│   └── dto/
└── infrastructure/{{moduleName}}/    ← adapters: controller, repository implementation
    ├── controllers/
    ├── persistence/
    └── {{moduleName}}.module.ts
```

Dependency rule: `infrastructure` → `application` → `domain`. Never the other way around.

The current repository implementation is in-memory (`{{moduleName}}InMemoryRepository`). To connect a real database, just write a new implementation of `I{{moduleName}}Repository` and swap it in `{{moduleName}}.module.ts` — since the use-cases only depend on the interface, nothing else needs to change.

